00:02:50.839 --> 00:02:57.080
Yeah. So now casting is the idea of can we estimate current counts of some reporting data

00:02:57.080 --> 00:03:01.720
based on what we know about like reporting delays. So this is a big problem in public health because

00:03:02.279 --> 00:03:06.759
you'll get reporting data that takes a while after someone gets sick. It has to go maybe

00:03:06.759 --> 00:03:10.839
through testing or it'll go to our local health jurisdiction first or requires additional

00:03:11.559 --> 00:03:15.479
follow-up and investigation. So there's this long delay between when someone actually gets sick and

00:03:15.479 --> 00:03:19.880
when we actually know about that case. And it causes this problem where it will always look

00:03:19.880 --> 00:03:25.800
like cases are declining, even though that's maybe not the case. So I was using now casting to model

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:32.600
that delay for pertussis or whooping cough to try and better understand where an outbreak of

00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:39.320
whooping cough was going and what we expected it to look like. It was very, very interesting to me

00:03:39.399 --> 00:03:46.440
because in your talk, you showed some really misleading plots, right? Like it's a plot over

00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:52.600
time where we're looking at the levels of something like pertussis in the population

00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:57.639
and it looks like it's going down. If somebody just looked at it at a glance, they would be like

00:03:57.639 --> 00:04:02.679
cases are going down. We're headed in the right direction. But it was just because there's a delay

00:04:02.679 --> 00:04:08.919
in collecting the information or processing it or whatever. And eventually that's going to change,

00:04:08.919 --> 00:04:15.160
which sort of reminds me of the like jobs numbers that get updated two or three months down the

00:04:15.160 --> 00:04:19.160
road, right? They'll be like, jobs are up. And then two months later, they'll be like, oh,

00:04:19.160 --> 00:04:22.760
just kidding. In May jobs were actually like way down. And now that we have this info,

00:04:23.320 --> 00:04:27.399
it's different. It's kind of like that, but much more dire because we're talking about

00:04:27.399 --> 00:04:35.880
