00:37:42.603 --> 00:37:43.603
for that.

00:37:43.723 --> 00:37:49.003
But yeah, they're interesting forecasting challenges, really challenging problems.

00:37:49.003 --> 00:37:51.922
But it's a nice way to, if you just want to start working with the data and try out and

00:37:51.922 --> 00:37:55.563
see what it would look like to do, it's very well set up to work with.

00:37:55.563 --> 00:37:56.563
Cool.

00:37:56.563 --> 00:38:01.642
I'm going to add the link to COVIDNet in there and let me see if I can find FluNet as well,

00:38:01.642 --> 00:38:05.042
or maybe Isabella can find it.

00:38:05.042 --> 00:38:08.882
And then we had a couple of people drop some of the other things that Allie mentioned there,

00:38:08.882 --> 00:38:12.082
like data.wa.gov from Andy Cooper.

00:38:12.082 --> 00:38:13.082
Thank you.

00:38:13.562 --> 00:38:20.043
Jason had added data.gov, overarching data.gov, which is an amazing resource.

00:38:20.043 --> 00:38:26.082
And the data portal on data.gov is where I go for a lot of stuff because there are state

00:38:26.082 --> 00:38:31.202
organizations, municipalities, there's all kinds of stuff in the data portal.

00:38:31.202 --> 00:38:36.002
And then everybody hop in the chat and just take a look at some of the answers that people

00:38:36.002 --> 00:38:41.483
are giving, connect with people, chat with them more, ask them questions.

00:38:41.483 --> 00:38:44.522
Dylan says, I'm very interested in hearing these answers because I'm a data science professor

00:38:44.522 --> 00:38:46.522
and I want to help my students.

00:38:46.522 --> 00:38:47.882
We all want to help students.

00:38:47.882 --> 00:38:54.562
We want everybody to learn good things and have good takeaways.

00:38:54.562 --> 00:38:59.882
Prajesh had said for PII redaction that they have cloud plugins as well.

00:38:59.882 --> 00:39:02.483
And there was a question around AI.

00:39:02.483 --> 00:39:07.082
So I'd like to transition, let's use Prajesh's comment to transition to a question that might

00:39:07.122 --> 00:39:11.562
be posed to both Ali and Paul.

00:39:11.562 --> 00:39:17.723
Paul, I put it for Paul in the chat, but I don't know if Paul's going to have a bunch of.

00:39:19.283 --> 00:39:20.603
Oh, no, Paul did respond.

00:39:20.603 --> 00:39:26.642
OK, so the question was, how do you think about where you invest limited resources in

00:39:26.642 --> 00:39:29.483
this time of manufactured scarcity and public health?

00:39:29.483 --> 00:39:35.363
I have concerns about investing in these marketed as AI tools when we could instead be using

00:39:35.363 --> 00:39:39.762
financial resources to rehire and retain public health professionals.

00:39:41.163 --> 00:39:48.043
And so Paul had said, like, this requires careful assessment and having a really grounded

00:39:48.043 --> 00:39:52.483
understanding in data science that can give benchmarking comparisons to see how things

00:39:52.483 --> 00:39:53.363
really perform.

00:39:54.642 --> 00:40:00.082
And so far, the best value lies in the small admin and the bureaucratic type tasks.

00:40:00.243 --> 00:40:06.762
So, Ali, where does AI succeed in your eyes and where is it maybe falling short as far as

00:40:06.762 --> 00:40:07.283
public health?

00:40:08.163 --> 00:40:15.243
Yeah, I think advantage of AI is often like a scaling question of places where I've seen it do

00:40:15.243 --> 00:40:20.202
interesting work is around like digitizing records where it's like thousands and thousands of

00:40:20.202 --> 00:40:24.963
records in this work that just wouldn't happen because it's just like too much manual work

00:40:24.963 --> 00:40:28.882
that is not feasible and there's just not the resources to do that.

00:40:29.122 --> 00:40:30.803
So I think there's interesting opportunities there.

00:40:31.682 --> 00:40:37.522
I think I would always prioritize people over AI, especially at a time of limited resources.

00:40:39.043 --> 00:40:41.603
Yeah, that would be always my preference.

00:40:42.483 --> 00:40:46.963
But I think, yeah, for that, like scaling, and I think it's often the work that wouldn't happen.

00:40:46.963 --> 00:40:51.843
I think there's some work where it's like that was the goal with our pilot around the

00:40:51.843 --> 00:40:57.682
overdose reporting is that this is really time intensive manual work that has to happen and is

00:40:57.762 --> 00:40:59.363
keeping people from other work.

00:40:59.363 --> 00:41:04.322
And I'd love to be able to replace more of that because it's like, yeah, just to do these kind

00:41:04.322 --> 00:41:10.483
of standardized reporting things is, yeah, it's like high priority, it has to happen, but it's

00:41:10.483 --> 00:41:15.923
very time intensive and it keeps people from being able to look at things deeper and do the

00:41:15.923 --> 00:41:17.522
further work that we would like to be doing.

00:41:17.522 --> 00:41:20.243
So I think it's always like it's ideally a partnership.

00:41:20.243 --> 00:41:22.723
I think you really need that human review and knowledge.

00:41:22.723 --> 00:41:26.723
So I think it really, really is a partnership, but I would like to be able to take more of that

00:41:27.202 --> 00:41:28.882
that work away from people.

00:41:29.522 --> 00:41:31.442
Fantastic stuff that's getting in the way.

00:41:31.442 --> 00:41:36.403
And that is not maybe bringing anybody a ton of value, personal value to do it themselves.

00:41:37.442 --> 00:41:39.522
Okay, we had an anonymous question.

00:41:41.522 --> 00:41:46.723
And I know that Ali, you said that you were coming from cancer research, right?

00:41:46.723 --> 00:41:49.762
Not specifically public health as a background.

00:41:49.762 --> 00:41:54.483
This person is asking for someone who's interested in starting a career in public health with a

00:41:54.483 --> 00:41:55.762
background in science.

00:41:55.762 --> 00:42:00.243
With a background in statistics, data science and AI, how would you recommend breaking into

00:42:00.243 --> 00:42:00.803
the field?

00:42:00.803 --> 00:42:05.762
Specifically, how would you recommend gaining that domain knowledge to be qualified to break

00:42:05.762 --> 00:42:06.403
into the field?

00:42:07.043 --> 00:42:11.603
Is there anything specific that you did to kind of change your trajectory?

00:42:11.603 --> 00:42:17.442
Yeah, I think it was definitely a bit of a different time when I broke into public health.

00:42:17.442 --> 00:42:22.002
It was, yeah, it was during COVID and there was just really, really that need for additional

00:42:22.002 --> 00:42:28.562
data science support and to build out systems that could scale and just how do you deal

00:42:28.562 --> 00:42:33.122
with more data and topics around like moving to the cloud and things like that.

00:42:34.403 --> 00:42:38.803
I think those are still like all current problems that we face and are still relevant.

00:42:40.002 --> 00:42:42.483
But it was like some of those were kind of newer problems.

00:42:42.483 --> 00:42:46.163
And so there was definitely more room to break in from a computer science background.

00:42:46.163 --> 00:42:50.082
And yeah, computational biology, similar skills.

00:42:50.082 --> 00:42:50.803
That says it that way.

00:42:50.803 --> 00:42:55.603
So I think you can always take your skills and they will be a value.

00:42:56.963 --> 00:43:00.002
I think I always was just like personally interested in public health and I was really

00:43:00.002 --> 00:43:01.043
interested in infectious disease.

00:43:01.043 --> 00:43:03.363
So I was always just reading a lot about that.

00:43:03.363 --> 00:43:07.682
And it's like good to get, you can do little projects of getting started and trying out

00:43:07.682 --> 00:43:08.803
working with public health data.

00:43:10.243 --> 00:43:14.882
It's great way to, you know, get your feet wet of like, what does it mean?

00:43:14.882 --> 00:43:17.122
What does it look like to work with public health data?

00:43:18.002 --> 00:43:19.202
Yeah, get excited about it.

00:43:19.202 --> 00:43:20.642
Go read publications and papers.

00:43:20.642 --> 00:43:26.243
I feel like if you get the chance to talk to somebody, your passion for something and

00:43:26.243 --> 00:43:30.243
your interest in something will come through in your conversations with them because you

00:43:30.243 --> 00:43:35.843
won't be the one asking really, really high level questions.

00:43:35.843 --> 00:43:37.762
You'll be asking more nuanced questions, right?

00:43:37.762 --> 00:43:40.723
You'll be asking better questions and it will show through.

00:43:40.723 --> 00:43:47.202
In our episode with Alex Gold that we had a couple of years ago now, maybe a year ago,

00:43:47.202 --> 00:43:51.283
he focused on like his hiring decisions being talking with people who are asking really

00:43:51.283 --> 00:43:53.442
good questions and what a good question looks like.

00:43:54.562 --> 00:43:59.682
And so I feel like if you want to get to the good questions, you have to go do the research

00:44:00.803 --> 00:44:02.723
and be really interested in the domain.

00:44:03.442 --> 00:44:05.043
That was a fantastic anonymous question.

00:44:05.043 --> 00:44:08.403
And I have another one that I think is so fun that I just saw pop up.

00:44:08.403 --> 00:44:09.283
Put it in the chat here.

00:44:09.283 --> 00:44:15.122
It says, what are the top five R packages besides the tidyverse that you use regularly

00:44:15.122 --> 00:44:16.322
for Epi projects?

00:44:16.403 --> 00:44:17.442
I'll put you on the spot.

00:44:17.442 --> 00:44:18.082
We'll let you know.

00:44:18.082 --> 00:44:19.202
Okay, top five.

00:44:19.202 --> 00:44:19.442
Okay.

00:44:21.603 --> 00:44:22.882
So I've been doing a lot of nowcasting.

00:44:22.882 --> 00:44:27.283
I'm a big fan of the Epi nowcast and baseline nowcast packages.

00:44:27.283 --> 00:44:33.363
They're really well implemented and good ways to get started with nowcasting and have various

00:44:33.363 --> 00:44:37.923
levels of like the most basic models to like you can do a lot of complexity in your models

00:44:37.923 --> 00:44:43.122
in terms of like how fine grained of like different patterns you're looking for and

00:44:43.522 --> 00:44:46.882
incorporating like day-to-week effects and all these different reporting kind of patterns

00:44:46.882 --> 00:44:47.682
that happen.

00:44:47.682 --> 00:44:48.483
So I like those.

00:44:50.082 --> 00:44:51.043
Record linkage.

00:44:52.642 --> 00:44:57.202
I guess I've done a different, not always are, but fuzzy join is always a good way.

00:44:57.202 --> 00:45:03.043
And I will put a plug in for one of my colleagues made a Rust implementation of fuzzy join called

00:45:03.043 --> 00:45:03.682
fuzzy join.

00:45:03.682 --> 00:45:04.163
That's cool.

00:45:05.122 --> 00:45:05.843
It's really fast.

00:45:05.843 --> 00:45:07.363
So that's an interesting one to work with.

00:45:08.243 --> 00:45:09.043
I'll share that one.

00:45:09.682 --> 00:45:12.483
I've enjoyed recently DizNetwork.

00:45:12.483 --> 00:45:18.803
I was like, yeah, working with some like tree data and I was looking at like employee data

00:45:18.803 --> 00:45:19.603
like in structures.

00:45:19.603 --> 00:45:24.002
And so it's a great, it was so nice for interactive, like tree and graph visualizations.

00:45:25.522 --> 00:45:27.442
Yeah, I really enjoyed that.

00:45:27.442 --> 00:45:30.322
And it made it a lot easier to look at the data that I was working with.

00:45:32.403 --> 00:45:40.403
Yeah, I think, yeah, I'm going to add fuzzy join, which is from John Downs.

00:45:40.403 --> 00:45:44.963
He gave a presentation about it at Cascadia Conf this year, which I freaking loved.

00:45:44.963 --> 00:45:49.202
And I am trying to get John to come on the data science lab to talk about the challenges

00:45:49.202 --> 00:45:54.163
of fuzzy joins, how fuzzy join as a package solves that problem.

00:45:54.803 --> 00:45:59.122
John, if you're listening, Ali, go tell John, reply to Libby's email.

00:45:59.122 --> 00:46:02.642
Come on the data science lab with Isabella and Libby.

00:46:02.642 --> 00:46:03.682
Yes, waka waka.

